{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And he sent.</b> One must examine the verse's saying <b>before him</b> — seemingly unnecessarily. Also, why was it necessary to say the word <b>his brother</b>, for it is well known that Esav is his brother. Also, why was it necessary to mention <b>to the land of Seir, the field of</b> etc., for the mission would not change whether he is there or in another place. And further, one must examine its saying <b>to the land</b> (אַרְצָה) and not saying 'to the land' (לְאֶרֶץ), and although the Sages, may their memory be for a blessing, said (Yevamot 13b) that wherever a ל mem is required etc. — nevertheless it is a thing that causes a change in meaning." "<b>Indeed,</b> the intent of the verse is to give a reason for its saying <b>and he sent messengers</b> — why should he use the ministers on high unnecessarily, for he could do the matter through human servants, men of standing? Therefore it says <b>before him</b> — meaning, because they stand before Him, for he was permitted to use them for whatever he needed them in a matter that he could not accomplish through human beings. And it says that they were needed because the mission was to Esav, and he is a great man, as is known according to their words, may their memory be for a blessing (Genesis Rabbah 75), and the mission of others — it is possible he would not consider them worth answering back. Or it is possible that he would strike them first without answering and come upon Yaakov like a swooping eagle, which is not the case with the angels. Also, in the midst of their mission his heart would tremble and expand in fear upon seeing the host of heaven, as they said, may their memory be for a blessing (ibid.): ״some were clothed in fire and rode horses״ etc. — until here — whereby he would fear to do evil." "And its saying <b>to his brother</b> — meaning: the reason for his effort in this mission, in the way of peace and conciliation, is because he was his brother, and he feared lest he not engage him in battle — perhaps the merit of his fathers would stand for him." "<b>Or</b> it may intend this way: <b>to Esav</b> — when he is a hater; <b>to his brother</b> — when he is not a hater. Meaning, that they should perform the mission according to the manner in which they find him: if they find him to be a hater, the mission would be in one manner, and if they see that he is his brother, the mission would be in another manner. And this matter could not be understood by anyone other than the angels, for human beings could be deceived." "<b>Or</b> it may intend that the meaning is to precede — to send his emissaries before Esav would learn from others that Yaakov had come, along the lines of what they said, may their memory be for a blessing (Sotah 41b): ״precede the wicked one״ etc. Therefore he chose to send the angels, so they would not need to travel — and in the place where they are, there they would perform their mission, for the entire world is within their four cubits. And that is its saying <b>and he sent messengers</b> — the meaning of <b>before him, to Esav</b> etc. — namely: they are still standing before him and they perform their mission to Esav his brother, even though he is in a distant place. And that is its saying <b>to the land of Seir</b> (אַרְצָה) — and therefore it did not say 'to the land' (לְאֶרֶץ), for they would not need to go but would turn their faces and speak with Esav, and standing in their place they would return the answer to him — understand this. And its saying <b>field of Edom</b> — meaning: before Esav would leave his place, they would reach him with the mission, for the place of his dwelling is called the field of Edom." "<b>And by way of remez</b> (hint), Scripture intends to allude to three periods during which the children of Esav will change: The first — Jacob and Esav will be in the trait of brotherhood, and that is its saying <b>Esav his brother</b>, and this was until the time of the Destruction. And even though there was a time when Edom was under the hand of Israel, that was a brief time. And the second — from the time the Temple was destroyed until the time of the end, when Esav is in a high elevation and the eminence of Israel is not visible before him, and this is what he alluded to by saying <b>to the land of Seir</b> with a pataḥ under the alef, to show an exalted rank. And the third — in the days destined for us, when Edom will be a heritage, and that is its saying <b>field of Edom</b>, like a field that is plowed."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:45:46.286850958Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}